INT. NIGHT MARKET FISH STALL - 3 A.M.

Crushed ice, tin tubs, and a hanging bulb swinging in the draft of the refrigeration fans. Silver bodies glisten in rows. A radio plays something thin and old.

INSPECTOR HONOR MBEKI, forties, in a gray trench, stands under the bulb with her hands in her pockets. Across the counter, WEI-LING CHEN, sixties, a fishmonger in rubber boots, guts a snapper with a short, bright knife. Between them, on the ice, a plastic bag with something heavy and dark inside. Behind the stall, half-hidden by the tarp, KENDRICK "KEN" DOYLE, thirties, in a hoodie, watches and chews a thumbnail. In the far corner, ZSOFIA SZABO, seventies, who sells the next stall's lemons, sits on an upturned crate peeling one with her thumbnail, apparently asleep.

MBEKI
Mrs. Chen. This bag was in your ice.

WEI-LING
Everything's in my ice.

MBEKI
Is it yours?

WEI-LING
(not looking up)
I sell fish.

The knife keeps moving. Ken shifts his weight. The tarp rustles. Mbeki's eyes flick to it, and back.

MBEKI
(to the stall)
Anyone else want to claim it?

Silence. Zsofia's lemon peel falls in a single long spiral.

KEN
(muttering)
Not mine.

MBEKI
I didn't ask you, Mr. Doyle.

KEN
You said anyone.

MBEKI
So I did.

Wei-Ling lays the knife down, wipes her hands, and slides the bag toward Mbeki across the ice without breaking eye contact. Ken starts forward, stops. Zsofia opens one eye. Mbeki takes the bag, weighs it, and sets it down.

MBEKI (CONT'D)
(softly)
Mrs. Chen. Open it.

WEI-LING
Why?

MBEKI
Because it's yours.

Wei-Ling's face doesn't move. But her hand, reaching for the zipper, trembles. She opens it. Inside: a heavy iron key ring, dozens of keys, and a folded photograph of a young man.

She looks at the photo for a long time.

WEI-LING
That is my son.

Ken drops his thumb from his teeth. Zsofia, quietly, sets down the lemon.
